\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Mayor Howorth reported that she attended the Independent Cities Association Conference this past weekend in San Diego. She found the conference informative, with topics including efforts to bring film production back to Los Angeles and how cities can revise policies to make certain processes more accessible to residents. Another key topic focused on natural disaster preparedness. Mayor Howorth also spoke with a representative from Waste Management, one of the event sponsors, and took the opportunity to raise concerns regarding the recent service outage and the underground utility district. She noted that she and City Manager Talyn Mirzakhanian will be meeting with the representative to further discuss these matters.\line\line Councilmember Tarnay also reported that she attended the Independent Cities Association Conference and found it valuable to connect with representatives from other cities to share ideas and learn from one another. She highlighted a particularly interesting session on how artificial intelligence can be used to streamline operations and enhance transparency and accessibility for residents.\line\line Councilmember Franklin also reported attending the Independent Cities Association Conference and found it valuable to meet Los Angeles County Supervisor Lindsey Horvath, who participated in a Q&A session and made time for one-on-one conversations at the end of the meeting. He noted that he raised concerns regarding homelessness and crime prosecution, and discussed potential collaboration with newly elected District Attorney Nathan Hochman.\line\line\pard Mayor Howorth also reported that representatives from the Governor\rquote s Office met today with her and City Manager Talyn Mirzakhanian to discuss housing. They also raised concerns regarding homelessness and crime prosecution, and received guidance and ideas to help address these issues.\cf1\i0\f1\par
